WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.899
So when a couple of months ago, we got these
really wild polls, nationally, polls that

00:00:04.899 --> 00:00:10.091
had Donald Trump down to Joe Biden by 10 points
nationally, by 12 points nationally, there

00:00:10.091 --> 00:00:13.890
were even a few that said Donald Trump was
down by 15 points nationally.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:20.920
I said at the time whether or not those polls
were accurate at the time and remember, accurate

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.630
means within the margin of error.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:26.999
So if it says fifteen, it could really be
eight depending on the margin of error.

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:33.550
I repeated at the time, it is very unlikely
that on Election Day we're going to see a

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.230
10 point margin in the popular vote.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.809
It's just extraordinarily unlikely.

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:40.310
I don't think any of us should be imagining
that that's the case.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:44.330
We should imagine it's going to be much tighter
and it means we all have to vote at the same

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:45.330
time.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.770
If these polls keep coming out, we can't really
ignore them.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:51.980
And indeed, there are now multiple new polls
that have Joe Biden up by 12.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.600
There is a new Reuters Ipsos poll which says
that Joe Biden leads Donald Trump by 12 points

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.140
nationally.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.999
That's among likely voters, 52 to 40.

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:04.949
There's also a new USC poll which says Joe
Biden is up by 12.

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:08.799
And importantly, in that one, Joe Biden is
polling at 53 percent.

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:14.060
So in both the Reuters Ipsos poll and the
USC poll, not only is Donald Trump losing

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.460
by double digits, but Joe Biden is over 50
percent support, which is an important metric

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:20.460
to keep an eye on.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.100
Now, in these polls, somewhere between three
and five percent of likely voters say they're

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.930
just undecided right now.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.549
Now, I've talked about this before.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:30.799
Who are those people?

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.579
How could you possibly be undecided at this
point unless you literally not paid attention

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:35.579
to politics?

00:01:35.579 --> 00:01:37.930
And in that case, how likely are you to actually
vote?

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.049
That's a question.

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:43.950
If we say the electorate is expected to be
about one hundred and thirty million people,

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.180
if five percent are undecided.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.620
That is six and a half million voters right
now.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:53.380
Six and a half million voters who are looking
at this situation and saying, I might vote

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.469
Biden or I might vote Trump.

00:01:56.469 --> 00:01:58.960
I continue to be stunned and appalled by that
reality.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.299
I would love to talk to some of those undecided
voters.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:02.930
I invite you to call in.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:05.119
I've not heard from any of them so far.

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:12.680
Now, it's really important to remember that
a popular vote margin correlates differently

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.090
to the likelihood of an electoral victory
for Democrats versus Republicans.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:24.150
There is a structural imbalance against Democrats
where, as you know, sometimes Democrats win

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.320
the popular vote and don't become president
because they lose the Electoral College, as

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.500
I'm sure you remember, unless you're four
years old, in which case I don't even know

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.739
that we have four year olds in our audience
in 2016.

00:02:36.739 --> 00:02:42.400
Donald Trump lost the popular vote by two
point one, but he won the Electoral College

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.540
thanks to 70 something thousand votes in three
states.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.920
Nate Silver from 538 did something important.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.220
He crunched the numbers to tell us, given
a particular popular vote margin.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.489
How likely is it that Joe Biden will win the
Electoral College?

00:02:58.489 --> 00:03:04.760
And again, understand that the numbers are
skewed in this way because of the urban rural

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:11.440
divide and because of some other sort of base
level factors that favor Republicans in the

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.909
Electoral College, even at the even while
being at a disadvantage in the popular vote.

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:25.909
So if Joe Biden wins the popular vote by under
one point, his odds of becoming president

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:30.900
of getting 270 electoral votes are only six
percent.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:37.140
If Joe Biden can win the popular vote by one
to two points, he has a 22 percent chance

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.900
of winning the Electoral College.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:44.140
If Joe Biden matches Hillary Clinton's popular
vote win somewhere in the two to three point

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:48.769
range, it is still slightly more likely that
Trump wins the Electoral College.

00:03:48.769 --> 00:03:53.740
In other words, if Biden gets a similar margin
of victory in the popular vote to Hillary,

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.630
he only has a 46 percent chance of winning
the Electoral College.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:03.740
That's why we all have to vote only when Joe
Biden's popular vote lead gets beyond three

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:04.740
points.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.939
Does Joe Biden become the likely candidate
to become president if he wins between three

00:04:07.939 --> 00:04:09.920
and four points in the popular vote.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.200
He has a 74 percent chance of being president.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:18.860
And as you can see, if Joe Biden were to get
a six point popular vote win.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:22.770
The odds of becoming president go above ninety
nine percent.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:30.170
If in some other universe Joe Biden actually
were to win the popular vote by 10.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:33.720
It would be all but certain that he becomes
the next president of the United States.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:40.920
So that's how I would be thinking about the
popular vote versus the electoral vote scenario.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:47.200
Now, let's look at Joe Biden's average lead
right now, because the 12 point lead is not

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.670
representative of the average.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:54.570
If you look at Joe Biden's average in all
national polls, he's up by six point nine.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:55.570
Okay.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:59.140
If Joe Biden were to win by six point nine,
as I just showed you.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.520
He will almost certainly win the Electoral
College, right?

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.100
Well, yes and no if he really wins by six
point nine, yes.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.950
But remember that there is a margin of error
here.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:15.500
Now, when you're averaging poll results, you
can't just say, OK.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.510
Biden's up by six point nine.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:21.880
If the average poll has a three percent margin
of error for each candidate, that means maybe

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.690
Joe's only up by zero point nine.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:31.020
There are some complex mathematics involved
in figuring out an overall margin of error

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:36.330
when you're already averaging poll results,
which in and of themselves have margins of

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:37.330
error.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:38.330
It doesn't matter.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:44.360
The point is Hillary Clinton ended up with
a popular vote win.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.860
That was two thirds the size of what the polling
predicted.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:49.690
So let me put it a different way.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.940
Hillary Clinton was expected to win by three
in the polls.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:54.220
She won by two.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:59.530
That means her real margin was two thirds
of the expected margin.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:05.791
If we apply that to Joe Biden, if we say,
OK, Biden is winning by six point nine, but

00:06:05.791 --> 00:06:08.950
we're only expecting him to win by two thirds
of that.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:13.480
That would be about a four point popular vote
margin of victory.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:20.390
If that were the case, it still doesn't guarantee
victory for Joe Biden, but it certainly starts

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:21.660
to look pretty good.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.580
What does this mean?

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:25.750
This means we all have to vote.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.610
That's really all that this means.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:32.310
And even though the popular vote numbers may
be looking pretty good, we do have to talk

00:06:32.310 --> 00:07:35.020
about something that's going on in Minnesota,
which is not looking quite so good.

